# Throughput floors for scripts/protocol-matrix.sh --floors (CI / Linux).
#
# Why this file exists: the matrix used to assert only `mbps > 0`, so a
# transport could run at a fraction of its own rate and still report PASS. KCP
# spent its whole life at 22.25 MiB/s (a ratio of 0.042 against tcp-plain) that
# way. A gate on `mbps > 0` cannot see that class of regression; a floor can.
#
# Format: `row=42` is an absolute MiB/s floor, `row=ratio:0.06` is a fraction of
# this run's own tcp-plain. Ratios are used wherever possible because the
# runner's absolute capacity moves between runs; tcp-plain itself has no
# denominator, so it takes an absolute floor.
#
# Calibration (2026-11-10, after #603 fixed the benchmark peer's Nagle timer):
# three compat runs on the shared runner — 42844190428, 42844263709,
# 42844271249 — measured tcp-plain at 636.1 / 1098.5 / 803.0 MiB/s (a 1.7x
# capacity spread) with every row's ratio agreeing within 1.3x:
#
#   row           a      b      c    ratio min/max
#   tcp-tls     406.6  738.1  617.0  0.639/0.768
#   tcp-tls-mux 348.6  638.9  594.6  0.548/0.740
#   ws-plain    496.6  914.5  633.3  0.781/0.832
#   ws-tls      362.4  671.8  582.0  0.570/0.725
#   ws-tls-mux  320.1  557.2  499.2  0.503/0.622
#   wss         363.4  660.4  550.2  0.571/0.685
#   kcp-plain    64.2  110.2  139.6  0.100/0.174
#   kcp-tls      91.0  144.3  181.7  0.131/0.226
#   kcp-tls-mux  38.8   54.3   72.1  0.049/0.090
#   quic        189.6  391.7  378.1  0.298/0.471
#
# Rule: every floor is half the LOWEST ratio observed for that row, rounded
# down to two decimals (0.3195 -> 0.31, 0.0245 -> 0.024) so a floor is never
# above half. That is a 2x regression budget per row — enough for a slow
# runner, tight enough to catch a halving.
#
# tcp-plain is not halved from a CI run: half of the slowest CI run would be
# 318, and the repo records this harness shape at 191-197 MiB/s on a Linux VM
# (docs/archive/notes/2026-11-10-bridge-buffer-64k-default.md, section 4), where
# 318 would fail a healthy machine. 150 is half of that observation, so the
# absolute floor fires only on a collapse, and the ten ratio floors carry the
# sensitivity.
#
# macOS: the WebSocket rows sit far lower there (1-stream 64 KiB ping-pong is
# ~2x slower on macOS by default), so this file would fire on a healthy Mac.
# Local runs use scripts/protocol-matrix.floors.macos; CI is Linux and uses
# this file.
#
# Caveat: these ratios predate #603 (the benchmark peer now sets TCP_NODELAY),
# so they were measured through a Nagle-taxed peer and are therefore cautious
# rather than tight. Re-derive from a fresh set of runs; do not rescale.
#
# Self-check: a floor that starts firing on a healthy run is a floor that is
# too high. Lower it and record why, rather than deleting the row.

tcp-plain=150
tcp-tls=ratio:0.31
tcp-tls-mux=ratio:0.27
ws-plain=ratio:0.39
ws-tls=ratio:0.28
ws-tls-mux=ratio:0.25
wss=ratio:0.28
kcp-plain=ratio:0.05
kcp-tls=ratio:0.06
kcp-tls-mux=ratio:0.024
quic=ratio:0.14
